WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.140
Temporary files are not all the same.

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:10.360
A scratch file used during one calculation has different requirements from a cache that intentionally survives between runs.

3
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:16.060
A staging file for final output is different from a handoff file consumed by another component.

4
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.880
A diagnostic log has a different lifetime and audience than an intermediate processing file.

5
00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:25.800
Staging output often needs a clean transition from working state to final state.

6
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:32.060
Until the program has finished writing and validating the content, other code should not treat the temporary file as final.

7
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.320
This calls for clear naming, ownership, and finalization behavior.

8
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.760
Intermediate files may contain sensitive or incomplete content.

9
00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:50.780
A program might write decoded data, generated reports, transformed records, temporary exports, or data that is safe only after later validation.

10
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:58.160
The temporary step can be more sensitive than the final artifact because it may contain extra context or unredacted values.

11
00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.240
Handoff files need clear consumer expectations.

12
00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:13.020
If one component writes a file for another component to read, the contract should define where it appears, when it is complete, what permissions apply, how the consumer validates it, and who removes it.

13
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:17.460
Logs, caches, and spool files also need different controls.

14
00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.400
Logs may be retained.

15
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.420
Caches may survive restarts.

16
00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.120
Spool files may be observed by operational tools.

17
00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:31.540
The right controls depend on purpose, sensitivity, lifetime, sharing requirements, and cleanup behavior.
